IDENTIFYING documentation, last updated on 16 Sep 2026

How a user earns +r.  doc/readme.accounting describes the model -- an
account IS a nickname, +r means identified to the one in use -- and this
file describes the three ways in and the one thing behind them.

  AUTHENTICATE      IRCv3 SASL, for a client that negotiates it
  ACCOUNT           the same question as an ordinary command
  /msg NickServ     the same question again, for whoever is used to it

All three hand the credential to sasl_login_request(), and everything
after the credential -- asking the provider, granting +r, taking the
nickname, lifting the freeze -- is one path.  What differs between them
is only which numerics the client is answered in.


WHO DECIDES
===========

The server does not know whether a password is right.  It holds the
question and the answer's consequences; a module holds the answer.

  ircd/sasl.c            the shape of an exchange.  Turns AUTHENTICATE
                         lines into an authcid (the address), an authzid
                         (which of that address's accounts) and a
                         secret, and stops.  Knows nothing about
                         clients, which is why sasl_t can test the whole
                         state machine without a server.
  ircd/account.c         the provider register and the questions in
                         flight.  Never dereferences a struct Client.
  ircd/account_user.c    what an answer does to a user: account_login(),
                         account_logout(), the guest rename.
  ircd/m_authenticate.c  where the three meet, and the only place that
                         holds all three at once.
  ircd/m_account.c       the ACCOUNT command.
  modules/services/identity/    the provider: the store, the Argon2, the
                         cache in front of the database.
  modules/services/irc_services/  NickServ: the grace period and the
                         commands a person types.

One provider at a time.  A second registration is refused rather than
replacing the first, so two identity modules in ircd.conf produce an
error in the log instead of a coin flip.  With no provider registered
the sasl capability is not advertised at all: a client that negotiates
SASL against a server which cannot authenticate anybody would find out
after sending its password, which is worse than seeing that it is not
on offer.


SASL, ON THE WIRE
=================

    CAP LS
    :server CAP * LS :... sasl=EXTERNAL,PLAIN ...
    CAP REQ :sasl
    AUTHENTICATE PLAIN
    AUTHENTICATE +
    AUTHENTICATE <base64 of authzid NUL authcid NUL password>
    :server 900 maria maria!u@host maria :You are now logged in as maria
    :server 903 maria :SASL authentication successful
    CAP END

The capability's value is the mechanism list, sorted by name so it does
not depend on module load order.  The core brings two:

  PLAIN     RFC 4616.  Sends the password in the clear, so it carries
            SASL_MECH_NEEDS_TLS and is refused on a plain connection
            unless FEAT_ACCOUNT_REQUIRE_TLS is turned off on purpose.
  EXTERNAL  RFC 4422 appendix A.  The credential is the certificate the
            server already has; the message carries only which account.
            No certificate, no credential -- failing there rather than
            letting the provider decide keeps "authenticated as nobody"
            from existing even for an instant.

A module adds its own with module_add_sasl_mechanism(); registering or
unregistering one re-advertises the capability, with CAP NEW / CAP DEL
on a running server.

AUTHENTICATE has no P10 token, on purpose.  Every server runs the
identity module against the same store, so there is nothing to route
and no half-open session left on the far side of a netsplit; what
travels is +r, which travelled already.

A message longer than 400 characters is chunked, as the specification
says; a continuation is charged its bytes but not the flat per-command
flood penalty, or a chunked credential would cost two seconds per chunk
before the client had finished connecting.  Starting an exchange is
charged in full, and three consecutive failures end it.


DURING REGISTRATION, AND AFTER
==============================

A client that authenticates after registering is simply logged in.  One
that authenticates during registration cannot be: it is not a user yet,
and a user mode cannot be granted to something that is not a user.  So
the two halves are separated:

  - the nickname is taken at once (account_claim_nick()), so the client
    is introduced to the network under the name it is entitled to
    instead of joining as one thing and being renamed a moment later;
  - the 900 and 903 are sent at once.  A client waits for the 903
    before it sends CAP END, and CAP END is what lets registration
    finish: holding the numeric would be each side waiting for the
    other;
  - only the +r grant waits, until the end of register_user().

Registration is held meanwhile by AR_SASL_PENDING, one more flag beside
ident, DNS, CAP and the PING cookie -- registration was already a state
machine that waits, so this is not a new kind of thing.

If the exchange fails, the client comes in without +r and under
whatever name it is entitled to, which for a registered nickname it
could not prove is a guest name.


ACCOUNT
=======

    ACCOUNT LOGIN <address> <password> [<account>]
    ACCOUNT LOGOUT
    ACCOUNT LIST

For every client that does not speak IRCv3, and the same question
underneath.  A client that never negotiated the sasl capability is
never told about SASL: there is no 903, and a failure is ERR_ACCOUNTFAIL
(983) with the reason.

LOGIN works before registration as well as after.  Before, it behaves
like PASS and holds registration with the same AR_SASL_PENDING; after,
it identifies, or switches which account is in use, which is the same
act.  With no account named it takes the address's default one.

LIST shows the accounts of the address you are identified with:

    :server 984 maria maria       *d :in use, default
    :server 984 maria maria_movil -  :available
    :server 985 maria :End of ACCOUNT LIST

The second parameter is flags for a client -- "*" the one in use, "d"
the address's default, "-" neither -- and the text is for the person
and is translated.  Which one is in use is the server's own knowledge,
not the provider's.

LIST needs cli_user()->email, which exists only because this client
authenticated with it.  There is no form of this command that lists
somebody else's accounts: asking "what accounts does this address
hold" about an address you have not authenticated with is an account
enumerator, whether or not the answer is filtered afterwards.  Without
it, ERR_NOTAUTHENTICATED (986).

ACCOUNT has these three subcommands and does not grow.  Registering an
account, verifying an address or changing a password do not touch
struct Client: they are policy, and policy is NickServ's.

Like AUTHENTICATE it has no P10 token, for that reason and one more:
"AC" is what the historical ircu account burst used, and giving it a
different meaning here would land an old peer's burst on a client
command.


NICKSERV
========

    /msg NickServ IDENTIFY <address> <password> [<account>]
    /msg NickServ REGISTER <address> <password>
    /msg NickServ PASSWORD <old> <new>
    /msg NickServ DROP <password>
    /msg NickServ STATUS

IDENTIFY is the door a frozen client can still reach: a PRIVMSG to a
local +S bot is one of the few things the freeze lets through, and it
exists for this command.  With no account named and the client frozen
it means the nickname in use -- "identify to this nickname" is what the
client is being asked to do, and logging it in as some other account of
its would rename it out from under the question.

REGISTER takes the nickname in use and no other: an account is a
nickname, so registering one you are not wearing is registering a name
you have not shown you can hold.  It creates the address if it is new,
and if the address exists the password must be its password -- taking a
second nickname for an address is as much an act of its holder as
changing the password is.  It logs the client in on the spot, because
the password was just checked or just set.

PASSWORD and DROP need the address the client proved was its own.  DROP
ends in a logout, because +r to an account that no longer exists is not
a state the model defines.

"max_accounts" and "grace_period" go in the Service{} block that
already declares the bot, not in a block of their own:

    Service {
      name = "NickServ";
      type = "nickserv";
      "max_accounts" = 3;
      "grace_period" = 1 minutes;
    };


WHAT THE PROVIDER IS ASKED
==========================

include/account.h.  Four questions, all of them answered later, in the
main thread, and none of them allowed to block:

  ap_verify   is this credential good?
  ap_lookup   whose nickname is this?
  ap_list     what accounts does this address hold?
  ap_change   register / change the password / drop

All four are required.  A provider that could verify but not list would
make ACCOUNT LIST answer "the identity service is not available" on a
server where identity works, and that is the one answer a user cannot
tell from an outage.

Every question has a deadline (FEAT_ACCOUNT_TIMEOUT) on a timer of
account.c's own, is dropped when the client it was about leaves, and is
failed if the module is unloaded still owing it.

ap_lookup's three answers are FREE, REGISTERED and UNKNOWN, and UNKNOWN
is not FREE.  See WHEN NOTHING CAN BE ESTABLISHED in
doc/readme.accounting; it is the rule that overrides the rest.


THE IDENTITY MODULE
===================

modules/services/identity/.  No bot, no commands, not one line sent to
a user: it registers the provider and answers.  It owns the schema, so
it is a directory module with migrations, applied by an operator:

    /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY identity

It needs a Database{} block and the postgres module.  A Redis{} block
and the redis module are optional and only make it faster.  It runs
Argon2 on a worker, so FEAT_WORKER_THREADS must not be zero -- with no
worker pool it refuses every verification and says so in the log, which
is better than stopping the server for a quarter of a second per login.

APPLY THE MIGRATIONS BEFORE LETTING ANYBODY IN.  Until they are applied
there is no "account" table, so every nickname lookup fails, and a lookup
that fails is UNKNOWN, and UNKNOWN is not FREE -- so every user that
registers is renamed to guest-*.  That is the rule working exactly as
written (the source of truth is not answering), not a bug, and it is the
one moment a new deployment meets it: bring the server up, apply the
migrations, then open the port.

The password pepper comes from the IRCU_PASSWORD_PEPPER environment
variable, the same value on every server, and deliberately not from the
configuration file: it is the one secret whose whole purpose is to not
be wherever the hashes are, and a configuration file gets copied,
diffed and pasted into a bug report.

A verification is one query and then one worker.  The address's row and
the account it named come back together, before the password is
checked, and nothing about either is said until after it is: which
accounts an address holds is not something a wrong password gets to
find out.  For the same reason "no such address" and "wrong password"
are the same answer.

A stored hash made with costs below what the server asks for now is
made again -- on a worker, after the client has been answered, so that
raising the costs never shows up as login latency -- and the update is
guarded by the hash it replaces, so a password changed in between is
not overwritten.

A write takes an advisory lock inside a SQL function created by the
module's own migration, because counting an address's accounts and then
inserting one is a race the moment there are two servers.  See ONE
STATEMENT, AND WHAT TO DO WHEN THAT IS NOT ENOUGH in
doc/readme.database.


THE TRADITIONAL CLIENT
======================

An acceptance requirement, not an aspiration.

  - A client that does not ask for the sasl capability never sees an
    AUTHENTICATE.
  - ACCOUNT is one more command; a client that does not type it does
    not notice it.
  - +r already existed.  +f is one more letter in the MODE the client
    was already receiving.
  - There is still no account-notify, account-tag or extended-join, and
    now for a better reason than "they were removed": with an account
    that IS the nick, all three repeat the prefix.


TESTS
=====

  ircd/test/sasl_t.c        the mechanism register and the exchange,
                            including the chunking, without a server
  ircd/test/account_t.c     the provider register and the questions in
                            flight, without a provider
  ircd/test/crypto_t.c      Argon2, SHA-256, AES-GCM and base64 against
                            their published vectors
  tests/identity/           +f and what it blocks, the guest rename,
                            ACCOUNT's refusals, the capability with no
                            provider
  tests/identity_db/        the same thing with a provider: registering,
                            the account limit, identifying by all three
                            doors, the listing, the password change, the
                            drop, and the grace period against a real
                            store

tests/identity_db/ runs against the "identity" topology -- one ircd with
this module and one PostgreSQL, in docker-compose.yml.  There is no Redis
there on purpose: the cache is never the truth, so a server without one
answers exactly the same.  The schema is created by the tests themselves
running /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY identity, which is how a deployment does
it and therefore worth doing the same way.
